<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Honoré de Balzac</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1799">1799</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Honoré Balzac, Balzac</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Honoré de Balzac was a French novelist and playwright who is regarded as one of the founders of realism in European literature. He had his first success under his own name with Les Chouans (1829). In 1832 he conceived a series of books portraying all aspects of society, which became La Comédie humaine, a panorama of post-Napoleonic French life that includes Le Père Goriot (1835); it was unfinished at his death. His complex characters and his portrayal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> influenced writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcel-proust">Marcel Proust</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-dostoevsky">Fyodor Dostoevsky</a>. He married Ewelina Hańska in Berdychiv in March 1850 and died five months later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/honore-de-balzac.jpg" alt="Honoré de Balzac" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis-Auguste Bisson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:奥·巴尔扎克.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Balzac was born Honoré Balzac on 20 May 1799 to Bernard-François Balssa and Anne-Charlotte-Laure Sallambier. As an infant he was sent to a wet nurse, and when the children came home they were kept at a frosty distance from their parents, which affected him significantly. At ten he was sent to the Oratorian grammar school in Vendôme for seven years, where his father gave him little spending money and he became the object of ridicule among wealthier schoolmates. In 1820 he completed the five-act verse tragedy Cromwell, which his family found unimpressive. He started but never finished three novels: Sténie, Falthurne and Corsino.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the late 1820s Balzac tried several business ventures. A publishing house of cheap one-volume French classics failed, with many books sold as waste paper. He then tried a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> business and a type foundry, borrowed from family and friends, and was ruined by inexperience and lack of capital. He gave the businesses to a friend who made them successful but carried the debts for many years.</p><p>In 1832 he conceived a large series of books to paint &quot;all aspects of society&quot; and is said to have run to his sister's apartment crying, &quot;I am about to become a genius!&quot; It became La Comédie humaine, which included all the fiction he published under his own name. He wrote from one to eight in the morning, fuelled by black <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, and revised obsessively, covering printer's proofs with changes at great expense to himself and his publishers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Balzac's extensive use of detail, especially of objects, made him an early pioneer of literary realism. In the preface to Scènes de la vie privée he wrote that &quot;details alone will henceforth determine the merit of works&quot;. He was a legitimist, and his views were the opposite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>'s republicanism, yet his insight into working-class conditions won the esteem of socialists; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-engels">Engels</a> called him his favourite writer.</p><p>Critics compare him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-dickens">Charles Dickens</a>. Gustave Flaubert attacked his prose but praised his portrayal of society, writing, &quot;What a man he would have been had he known how to write!&quot; Henry James called him &quot;really the father of us all&quot;.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Honoré de Balzac” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
